186412368 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412368 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412368 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412368 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e5b90a83-101c-5227-b1b7-407300b1fd00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186412321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186412303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412369 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fec96176-6e4b-5b1f-af15-6a4b4b87e54e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1071 "$67\\text{ mod }6=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "025be531-fd73-5fa9-9ce4-f3e4c0c054c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412370 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $67$ modulo $6$ is the integer $0\\le r<6$ such that\n\n$$\n67≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $67$ as an integer multiple of $6,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $67≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$ because\n\n$$\n67=11\\cdot 6+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $67$ modulo $6$ is $1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}67 & ≡67-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡67-11\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡67-66 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $67$ modulo $6$ is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1071 "$-54\\text{ mod }7=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a2135af-f11d-5278-a560-c7cd73d93e86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412371 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $-54$ modulo $7$ is the integer $0\\le r<7$ such that\n\n$$\n-54≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-54$ as an integer multiple of $7,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-54≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$ because\n\n$$\n-54=(-8)\\cdot 7+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-54$ modulo $7$ is $2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-54 & ≡-54+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡-54+8\\cdot 7 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡-54+56 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-54$ modulo $7$ is $2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1071 "$-21\\text{ mod }5=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24b40dbb-8c36-5294-b359-e391ac1e418a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412372 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is the integer $0\\le r<5$ such that\n\n$$\n-21≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-21$ as an integer multiple of $5,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-21≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$ because\n\n$$\n-21=(-5)\\cdot 5+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is $4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-21 & ≡-21+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡-21+5\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡-21+25 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-21$ modulo $5$ is $4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a$ be a postive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ee231c9-5634-53c5-9930-57fd0fc0e5d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412373 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $b=12a+11.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $b:$ \n\n$$\nb=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=12a+11,$ and \n\n$$\n12≡2,\\,11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 12a+11 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 2a+1 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11$ is the following:\n\n$$\n2a+1\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412369 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213325" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives a two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $215a.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39ab88df-75d5-5304-96dd-f494bc2a5583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412374 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $b=215a.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{4},$ ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last four digits of the number $a:$ \n\n$$\na=…{d}_{4}{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+1000{d}_{4}+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $100:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ≡100(⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{4}+{d}_{3})+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡0+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\  & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1}$ is the two-digit number consisting of the last two digits of $a.$\n\nFinally, since $b=215a,$ and \n\n$$\n215≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,100)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 215a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100) \\\\ 15a & ≡10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,100)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $215a$ is the following:\n\n$$\n15a\\,\\text{mod}\\,100\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-102405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1071 "$39\\text{ mod }4=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412375 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n39≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $39$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $39≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n39=9\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & ≡39-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-9\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-36 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1071 "$-9\\text{ mod }4=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "549f3a8c-907a-5b2d-8f07-7849e3e239ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412376 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n-9≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-9$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-9≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-9=(-3)\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & ≡-9+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-9+3\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-9+12 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-9$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1071 "$-7\\text{ mod }2=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d645eda-2214-5faa-a63b-2fee859e4238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412377 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is the integer $0\\le r<2$ such that\n\n$$\n-7≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-7$ as an integer multiple of $2,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-7≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$ because\n\n$$\n-7=(-4)\\cdot 2+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is $1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7 & ≡-7+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡-7+4\\cdot 2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡-7+8 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\  & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-7$ modulo $2$ is $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186412347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1071 "$27\\text{ mod }4=${{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7119d9e9-8c81-54dd-a944-533c5aa1aed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412378 #edb/ref 1074 "The residue of $27$ mod